<!--
  ============================================================================
  RUT_PropaneLake.xml                    UtinniPatches · authored 2026-09-06
  ============================================================================
  LIQUID_BIOMES_MAP_1 — OFFLINE HALF. The item's fourth liquid biome, the
  propane lake, per design/Jawa/worldbuilding/biomes/the_propane_lakes.md.
  🔴 NOT painted onto any tile by this file or this pass — the lake's exact
  tile subset is explicitly owner-gated by render (the_propane_lakes.md's
  own "Owed" list: "LIQUID_BIOMES_MAP_1 — the lake's worldmap tiles under
  Umbra"; the item text: "the lake proper is a subset to rule with the
  owner by render (worldview.py) before painting"). This def exists so that
  subset has somewhere to go once it is ruled — nothing more.

  🔴 A key finding this reconciliation pass turned up, worth stating
  explicitly because it changes what "the propane lake" even means
  mechanically: AB_PropaneLakes (Alpha Biomes, sarg.alphabiomes) is a LAND
  biome — canBuildBase=true, isWaterBiome=false, impassable=false (read
  from the live def dump this pass, defs.sqlite mods=596/cec112bad2152f47,
  captured=2026-09-05T14:41:26Z) — not a world-map water biome like
  Ocean/Lake. Its "liquid propane lake" is delivered as a LOCAL-MAP terrain
  patch via its own terrainPatchMakers (perlinFrequency 0.015, fertility
  0.76-999 -> AB_PropaneLake terrain; 0.56-0.76 -> AB_SolidPropane), so
  every one of the 558 Umbra tiles painted AB_PropaneLakes today already
  generates lake-and-crust patches on ANY colony built there. That is a
  real, working liquid feature and this item does not touch it.
  What the_propane_lakes.md §0 measured as MISSING is a different, bigger
  thing: an actual WORLD-MAP water tile (water=1 in
  world/ASHKARR_WORLDMAP_tiles.csv) — Umbra currently reads water=0 on all
  802 of its tiles (measured this pass, both AB_PropaneLakes' 558 and
  BMT_CrystalCaverns' 244). A world-tile lake, the kind that shows blue on
  the planet view the way the Scald and the two brine seas already do,
  needs an isWaterBiome BiomeDef the way Ocean/Lake are — AB_PropaneLakes
  cannot become one without losing everything that makes it settleable.

  🔴 NOT ParentName="AB_PropaneLakes" and not ParentName="Lake"/"Ocean" for
  the same reason RUT_TheScald.xml/RUT_TwilightSea.xml give: Lake and Ocean
  carry no Name="..." attribute on their own <BiomeDef> tag (verified by
  direct read, Core/Defs/BiomeDefs/Biomes_Water.xml), and AB_PropaneLakes is
  the wrong shape to inherit from regardless (it is a land biome, not a
  water one) — so this restates the Ocean/Lake water-biome shape directly,
  the same pattern as the other three defs this item authors.

  Water terrain: REUSES the donor's own AB_PropaneLake / AB_SolidPropane
  terrain defs rather than inventing new ones — unlike the Scald, which
  needed a genuinely new mechanic (the boiling-lift burn), the propane
  lake's distinct liquid ALREADY EXISTS as a shipped, working TerrainDef
  (waterBodyType None — correctly not recognised as drinkable water by
  DBH or anything else, matching "propane, not water"; tags ['Water'],
  Impassable, pathCost 300, avoidWander true, canFreeze false — read off
  the live dump this pass). waterDeepTerrain -> AB_PropaneLake (the liquid
  centre); waterShallowTerrain -> AB_SolidPropane (the frozen margin crust,
  Standable, pathCost 12) — physically the correct pairing for a phase-line
  lake (R-H6b: propane liquefies at −42 C, so the shallow/cold edge freezes
  solid while the deep centre stays liquid).

  fishTypes / wildAnimals: left EMPTY, documented. the_propane_lakes.md §4
  already names the lake's intended exotics qualitatively ("the V-wake
  creature and its kin — propane-native, agitated by pumping, attacking
  pawns and pipe on sight") but no PawnKindDef exists for any of them yet
  (checked the live dump: no def named anything resembling "V-wake" or
  "VWake"). Authoring that creature is separate, unowed work (the sheet's
  own "Owed" list — "the lake terrain (flammable, off-gassing into
  saturation)" and the engine-feasibility pass are listed as owed there
  too, ahead of any creature). AB_PropaneLakes' own donor fishTypes
  (VCEF_PropaneBag, VCEF_AbyssalEel, VCEF_GiantPolarPrawn etc., read off the
  live dump this pass) belong to the LAND biome's local-map fishing zones
  and are not copied here — this is a world-tile water biome, a different
  gameplay surface, and copying them without an owner ruling would invent a
  content decision, not just an engine reconciliation.

  texture: reuses Biomes/AB_PropaneLakes (the donor's own world-map icon)
  as a placeholder rather than inventing new art — a dedicated icon
  distinguishing "the lake proper" from the surrounding AB_PropaneLakes
  land tiles on the world map is real, owed art work, not done here.
  ============================================================================
-->
<Defs>

  <BiomeDef>
    <defName>RUT_PropaneLake</defName>
    <label>the propane lake</label>
    <description>A black mirror of liquid fuel under the brightest sky on the planet, ringed by a crust of the same compound frozen solid. Nothing hot has touched it since the war.</description>
    <workerClass>BiomeWorker_Ocean</workerClass>
    <isWaterBiome>true</isWaterBiome>
    <canBuildBase>false</canBuildBase>
    <!-- allowRoads TRUE by owner ruling 2026-09-07: "All of our biomes should allow roads." 91 authored road links crossed RUT_NightsideIce and were invisible - the links were structurally valid (asymmetric 0, nonAdjacent 0), the biome simply refused to draw them. -->
    <allowRoads>true</allowRoads>
    <allowRivers>false</allowRivers>
    <hasVirtualPlants>false</hasVirtualPlants>
    <!-- Owner 2026-09-07: the lake was INVISIBLE on the world map because this pointed at
         Biomes/AB_PropaneLakes - the very texture of the LAND biome it sits inside, so the
         lake was drawn identically to its own shore. BiomeDef has no colour or material
         override; `texture` is the only lever on world-map appearance. Jawa_SickWater is a
         dark slate-blue with brown mottling - a murky liquid that does not read as clean
         water, which is the point: this is propane, not water. -->
    <texture>World/Biomes/Jawa_SickWater</texture>
    <impassable>true</impassable>
    <isBackgroundBiome>true</isBackgroundBiome>
    <waterDeepTerrain>AB_PropaneLake</waterDeepTerrain>
    <waterShallowTerrain>AB_SolidPropane</waterShallowTerrain>
    <wildAnimals />
    <coastalWildAnimals />

    <!-- GRAVTIDE SUPPORT, added 2026-09-07 (UNDERWATER_BIOME_SUPPORT_1).
         GravTide's arrival gate is generic - OceanArrivalPatches.IsOceanTile and
         OceanTileFeatures.IsWater both test PrimaryBiome.isWaterBiome - so this sea
         already qualifies for a gravship landing and a dive. What it lacked is the
         three things GravTide patches onto vanilla Ocean/Lake BY defName, which never
         reach a modded biome:
           1. terrainsByFertility - THE CRITICAL ONE. MapGenUtility.TerrainFrom() finds
              no terrain without it, logs "No terrain found in biome <X>" and falls back
              to TerrainDefOf.Sand, which is why an unpatched water biome generates an
              all-SAND map instead of a sea.
           2. baseWeatherCommonalities - otherwise every landing logs
              "All weather commonalities were zero. Defaulting to Clear."
           3. wildAnimalsCanWanderInto false - land animals have nowhere to walk from.
         Depth comes from NEGATIVE elevation (OceanTileFeatures.DepthOf = max(0,-elev));
         these tiles sit at -350 m, so ~350 m: past the ~150 m high-pressure nervous
         syndrome threshold, inside drysuit range (800 m). -->
    <wildAnimalsCanWanderInto>false</wildAnimalsCanWanderInto>
    <baseWeatherCommonalities>
      <Clear>14</Clear>
      <Fog>6</Fog>
    </baseWeatherCommonalities>
    <terrainsByFertility>
      <li>
        <terrain>AB_PropaneLake</terrain>
        <min>-999</min>
        <max>999</max>
      </li>
    </terrainsByFertility>
  </BiomeDef>

</Defs>
